# Personal Calendar — MCP Server

Exposes the Personal Calendar app (events, reminders/notifications, integrations) as an
MCP server over Streamable HTTP, so external applications (e.g. your friend's app) can
read and manage the calendar.

## Environment variables

Copy this file to `.env` and fill in the values:

| Variable | Description |
|---|---|
| `PORT` | HTTP port (default `8787`) |
| `MCP_API_KEY` | Shared API key that client apps must send to authenticate |
| `SUPABASE_URL` | Your Supabase project URL |
| `SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY` | Supabase service-role key (server-side only, keep secret) |
| `MCP_USER_ID` | Supabase **user UUID** whose calendar is exposed |

### Getting MCP_USER_ID

Run this SQL in the Supabase SQL Editor:

```sql
select id, email from auth.users;
```

The `MCP_USER_ID` is the `id` of the user you want your friend's app to access.

> Note: use the **service role key** (not the anon key). It is required so the server
> can read/write the calendar owner's rows despite RLS. Never expose it to clients.

## Run

```bash
npm install
cp .env.example .env   # then fill in values
npm start
```

Server starts at `http://localhost:8787/mcp`.

## How a client connects

The endpoint implements MCP **Streamable HTTP**. The client must send the API key in
every request via `Authorization: Bearer <MCP_API_KEY>` (or `X-API-Key: <MCP_API_KEY>`).

Example config for opencode (`~/.config/opencode/opencode.json`):

```json
{
  "mcp": {
    "personal-calendar": {
      "type": "remote",
      "url": "http://localhost:8787/mcp",
      "headers": { "Authorization": "Bearer {env:MCP_API_KEY}" }
    }
  }
}
```

Any MCP client can connect the same way (Claude Desktop, Cursor, custom apps, etc.).

## Available tools

| Tool | Description |
|---|---|
| `list_events` | List events, optional date range / status / category filters |
| `get_event` | Fetch one event by UUID |
| `search_events` | Search events by keyword in title/description/category |
| `create_event` | Create an event |
| `update_event` | Update fields of an event |
| `delete_event` | Delete an event (and recurring instances) |
| `check_conflict` | Check whether a time slot conflicts with existing events |
| `list_notifications` | List reminders/notifications (filter by event/status/channel) |
| `list_integrations` | Show status of Google/Outlook/WhatsApp connections |

## Deployment

### Vercel

This folder is Vercel-ready: the serverless function is `api/mcp.js` and the
route is exposed at `/mcp` via `vercel.json`.

1. Push the repo (or just this folder) to GitHub.
2. In Vercel: **Add New > Project**, import the repo.
3. Set **Root Directory** to `mcp-server`.
4. Add these **Environment Variables**: `MCP_API_KEY`, `SUPABASE_URL`,
   `SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY`, `MCP_USER_ID`.
5. Deploy. Your endpoint will be `https://<your-project>.vercel.app/mcp`.

> Note: the root `vercel.json` of this repo belongs to the Vite frontend. To
> deploy the MCP server separately, use `mcp-server` as the root directory in
> its own Vercel project.

### Other Node hosts

Any Node host works (Render, Railway, Fly.io, a VPS). Set the env vars and run
`npm start` — it serves Streamable HTTP at `http://<host>:8787/mcp`.

Then share the public URL + API key with your friend's app.
